\description{
Ultrasound tongue imaging of GOOSE + /l/ and FOOT + /l/ in Southern
British English, from 20 speakers in two age groups. Each speaker reads 24
words four times. The /l/ is inside a morpheme (\emph{hula}, \emph{bully}), at the
end of a morpheme before a suffix (\emph{fool-ing}, \emph{pull-ing}), at the end of a
word before a vowel (\emph{fool it}) or at the end of a word before a consonant
(\emph{fool five}). \code{ld} places each frame on a scale from the clear /l/ and
front vowel of \emph{hula} to the dark /l/ and back vowel of \emph{fool five}.
\emph{fool-ing} is much further along that scale than \emph{hula}; \emph{pull-ing} is
only slightly further than \emph{bully}, and more so for younger speakers. For
GAMMs and morphology-phonetics interactions.
