where \code{effect} is 0 for \code{morpheme_internal}, 2.6 (GOOSE) or 2 (FOOT)
for \code{word_prevocalic} and 4 for \code{word_preconsonantal}. For
\code{morpheme_final} it varies by speaker. For GOOSE it is uniform between
1 and 2.2. For FOOT it is 0.7 for four older speakers (\code{OF3}, \code{OF5},
\code{OF7}, \code{OM3}) and 0 for the other six; 0.8 for younger speakers, except
0.2 for \code{YF6}, 0 for \code{YM1} and -0.6 for \code{YF7}. With a Welch t-test of
\code{morpheme_internal} against \code{morpheme_final} on each speaker's token
means (p < 0.05), this gives the speaker counts reported in the paper:
a GOOSE contrast
for all 20 speakers; a FOOT contrast in the expected direction for 11
